\section{What it is not}{


By default it is limma's *legacy* estimator; \code{estimator = "marginal_likelihood"} is mzLib's own,
close to default limma's but not equal. \code{robust = TRUE}, the B-statistic and observation weights
are not implemented. With missing values a contrast's standard error is exact per feature, where
limma's \code{contrasts.fit()} approximates it, so on such data the contrasts differ from limma's by
design. \code{bh_adjusted} controls the false discovery rate among the features tested; it is not a
target-decoy q-value.

}
\section{Bridges before pyMzLib 0.6.0}{


Such a bridge has \code{stats fit} but ignores \code{--contrasts}, \code{--confidence} and \code{--estimator}. Rather
than return a fit that silently did not do what you asked, \code{stats_fit()} raises an
\code{mzlib_usage_error} naming the 0.6.0 bridge when you gave \code{contrasts} or \code{estimator} and the answer
lacks what they add.

}
